2.Explain the concepts of index, document, and shard in Elasticsearch.

Index:

An index in Elasticsearch is a collection of documents that share similar characteristics or belong to the same data category. It is analogous to a database in traditional relational databases.
Each index is identified by a unique name, typically lowercase and cannot contain special characters (except for "_").

document:

A document is the basic unit of information stored in Elasticsearch. It represents a single data record or entity, typically in JSON format.
Documents are stored within indices and are uniquely identified by a document ID.
Each document consists of one or more fields, where each field has a name and a value. Fields can be of different data types, such as text, keyword, numeric, date, and more.

shard:

A shard is a low-level data partition in Elasticsearch. It is a subset of an index's data and serves as the unit of scalability and parallelism.
When an index is created, Elasticsearch automatically divides it into multiple shards, which are distributed across the cluster's nodes.
Sharding allows Elasticsearch to distribute data and workload evenly across the cluster, improving performance and scalability.
There are two types of shards in Elasticsearch: primary shards and replica shards. Each index has a configurable number of primary shards, which are responsible for indexing and storing data. Replica shards are copies of primary shards used for redundancy and high availability.

